Suttanipāta IV–V: views and the questions beyond (@buddhist-snp-octads-and-beyond: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhism, #source @buddhist-source-suttanipata, #philosophy @buddhist-an-ones-to-threes, #buddhism @buddhist-an-ones-to-threes) { r_3N0CA3FMXHET5AA7YMF998RRCW
**Read:** chapters IV–V, the Aṭṭhakavagga and Pārāyanavagga, in [The Suttanipāta](../sources/Suttanipata.lingua#r_2PQ69G8GRX5WRVMFS1VYZKXQMS), completing the discourse collection. Compare difficult verses with commentary, then revisit the text to see what the explanation adds or constrains.

Investigate claims about attachment to views, conceit, desire, and release. A criticism of clinging to an opinion is not automatically an assertion that every claim is equally true or that reasoning has no place. Equally, do not remove the force of a difficult passage by restating a familiar system without argument.

**Close study:** choose one question and answer from the final chapter and one related passage from the Octads. Defend a reading against a serious alternative using wording and context. Discuss dating or textual layers as questions requiring evidence, rather than assigning a single secure date to the whole collection.

**Check:** explain what the verses establish and where your interpretation depends on commentary. The full accompanying commentary remains available for sustained deepening.

**Continue:** [Aṅguttara I–III: distinctions worth retaining](AN-Ones-to-threes.lingua#r_6F270NS2RXQMDPF4RSMFZ367MV).

**Return:** [Buddhism: from the path to sustained inquiry](../Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE).
